ic-leaning district (D+2 PVI)", "Shaheen's cash and outside-spending edge over DiLorenzo", "Raters mostly call it Likely D, with two at Lean D", "Thin polling: one September UNH poll showing only a 4-point race"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a Democratic lean of roughly 8-11 points, but the lone UNH poll shows a tighter 4-point race and two raters downgrade the seat to Lean D, so the forecast sits between those views at about +7 Democratic. Shaheen's fundraising and super PAC support reinforce the Democratic edge in an open seat.", "watch": "A second independent poll or late outside spending could confirm whether the race is truly tightening toward a tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Chris Pappas (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "New Hampshire's 1st District is an open seat this year, with Chris Pappas leaving to run for Senate. Democrat Stefany Shaheen faces Republican Anthony DiLorenzo in a district that leans Democratic by about two points. Shaheen has raised roughly $2.4 million and holds a cash advantage, boosted by outside spending including a super PAC that backed her in the primary; DiLorenzo has raised a similar amount but has less cash on hand.\n\nMost expert raters place the race in Likely Democratic territory, though Inside Elections and Sabato's Crystal Ball call it Lean Democratic. The only poll, a September University of New Hampshire survey of likely voters, showed Shaheen ahead 46 to 42, a narrower margin than fundamentals suggest. Markets price a Democratic win at about 95 percent. The race remains competitive, with cost-of-living issues and attacks over Trump and DiLorenzo's disclosures shaping the closing argument.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.937, "margin": 10.01}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9238, "margin": 9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9245, "margin": 9.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.8928, "margin": 7.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.8695, "margin": 7.5},